1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
From LiDAR observations to corridor insight.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:10,000
Choose Florida or Toronto. This demonstration uses Florida’s active data feed.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,960
Select an intersection, then zoom in to see moving road users.

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:23,960
Road users update from the BlueCity stream. Map positions and alignment are approximate.

5
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:30,880
Read received phase colors. A phase ID does not establish a verified movement direction.

6
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:36,960
Explore a saved one-hour recording. The rolling view also refreshes every 30 minutes.

7
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,880
Filter trajectories by travel direction and object type.

8
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:46,880
Choose intersections, then zoom around the pointer to inspect a specific time window.

9
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:53,240
Within one intersection, interrupted observations leave gaps.

10
00:00:53,240 --> 00:01:00,560
Enable local estimates: compatible same-session object IDs are connected with constrained curves.

11
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:05,960
Between intersections, sections of roadway are not observed by LiDAR.

12
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:13,480
Timing and motion help match candidate tracks. Dashed connections remain estimates.

13
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:21,720
Gray dashed lines show weaker hypotheses. Uncertain matches are excluded from measured KPIs.

14
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:29,880
Compare corridor traffic patterns: solid lines are observations; speed colors run from red to green.

15
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:37,640
The CTM explorer shows how demand and signal timing affect traffic queues.

16
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:44,400
Change the green duration and rerun the scenario to compute traffic propagation.

17
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:51,840
The CTM simulation is educational and has not been calibrated for field decisions.

18
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:59,200
Ouster Corridor Observatory—a traffic analytics research project by me.
